out.push(S('Stage 7R · Share'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Two counts and the band.** **Ten tests run: three load-bearing, five removable, and two where the reader could not tell the versions apart** — **and those two count as removable, which the room should hear said plainly.** **Then: what does each load-bearing paragraph hand the reader?** **A rule, a vocabulary, a measure or an objection, in nine of ten cases.** ⚠ **Then the ninety-word version, and the word _patronising_.**'));

// ===================== 23.5 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('23.5  A thousand words, and what the reader loses'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **A fifth of the piece, as a number of words.** **The fifths ran from a hundred and eighty to a thousand and forty** — **and the numbers stay on the board for the whole cycle, because a writer asked to cut _some_ cuts adjectives and a writer asked for a thousand and forty has to remove a paragraph, which is the only cut that changes a long piece.**'));
out.push(A('**b** Declared cuts: **2** and **4**. ⚠ **Each gives the words, the paragraph and the loss, and 4 names the reader to whom the loss matters.** **1, 3 and 5 are the three sentences almost every writer sends with a shortened piece** — **_tightened throughout_, _some cuts for length_, _considerably shorter_** — **and none of the three tells a reader what they are no longer being given.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The finding of this cycle is counter-intuitive and the room will miss it unless the teacher insists: a cut nobody notices should have been made before anybody asked, and a cut somebody notices is the only cut that was ever a decision.** **Of Mrs Tarabishi\'s thousand and forty words, about nine hundred and thirty were doing nothing and a hundred and ten were** — **and she had spent four years believing that cutting is the work of finding the nine hundred and thirty.** ⚠ **Then the mechanics, which the room will be grateful for: cut from the plan and not from the piece.** **Cross out lines on the one-page plan.** **Eleven minutes for a thousand words, and two hours on what the reader loses** — **and every student in this class said the second half was the harder, which is the sentence to end the cycle on.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **A paragraph nothing depends on** → **the reader loses it and nothing else; the loss is the writer\'s.**   **2** **A duplicate premise** → **the reader loses a reminder at the midpoint, which is real and is covered if 23.3 was done.**   **3** ⚠ **Ninety words of a repeated phrase** → **the reader loses nothing, and this is the only certain cut of the four.**   **4** **Nineteen hedges, four of them accurate** → **the reader loses the impression that you are cautious.**   **5** ⚠ **A loss that matters to one reader** → **name the reader.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
