out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Tarabishi has cut a thousand and forty words of five thousand two hundred, leaving four thousand one hundred and sixty, in four cuts: paragraph 11 at three hundred and eighty, paragraph 9 at three hundred and sixty, about ninety words of a repeated phrase, and two hundred and ten words of hedging, and she can say what the reader loses in each of the four, which took longer than the cutting did. On paragraph 11 the reader loses a study from 2011 that she likes and that nothing in the chapter depends on, which 23.4 proved with two readers and both versions, and the honest declaration is that the loss is hers and not the reader\'s. Mrs Oueidat said that on paragraph 9 the reader loses a reminder at the midpoint, which is a real loss and is not nothing, and whether it matters depends entirely on whether 23.3 was done. Mrs Tarabishi said it was, and that is why the cut is safe, because the advancing descriptions carry the premise forward in paragraphs 5, 7 and 8. Mr Shihabi asked which cut anybody noticed, and the hedging, which she had thought was safest: two hundred and ten words in nineteen instances, and one of her two readers said the chapter had become confident in a way she did not quite trust, and that reader is the one whose field it is. Mrs Khoury said that is the finding of the cycle and it is not about hedging, because a cut nobody notices should have been made before anybody asked, and a cut somebody notices is the only cut that was ever a decision. Dr Qabbani asked whether she puts some back, and four of the nineteen, in the places where she does not know. Mr Tayara asked how you cut without reading it all again, and you cut from the plan and not from the piece: cross out lines, which took eleven minutes. Mrs Rizk asked about two hours on four sentences, and Mrs Khoury said four sentences a supervisor will read instead of rereading the chapter. Mrs Ajouz asked what happens if the cut is wrong, and the declaration is what makes it recoverable, and her supervisor asked for paragraph 9 back and then withdrew the request. And Mrs Tarabishi said the thing she will take away is the proportions: of a thousand and forty words, nine hundred and thirty were never doing anything and a hundred and ten were, and she had spent four years believing that cutting was the work of finding the nine hundred and thirty, when the whole difficulty of it is the hundred and ten.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The proportions at the end are the finding and they overturn a belief the room shares.** **Of a thousand and forty words, nine hundred and thirty were doing nothing and a hundred and ten were** — **and four years of believing that cutting is the work of finding the nine hundred and thirty.** **Ask the room which of the two halves they have ever spent an hour on.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **_Tightened throughout_** → **a reader rereads the whole thing.**   **2** **A loss with a justification attached** → **it tells the reader which cut to argue about.**   **3** ⚠ **A loss with no reader attached** → **it is an opinion about the piece.**   **4** **Cutting from the piece and not the plan** → **eleven minutes becomes an afternoon.**   **5** ⚠ **The load-bearing paragraph cut** → **23.4; the test, not the feeling.**   **6** **Cut words not kept** → **a declared cut is recoverable or it is not declared.**'));
