out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Tarabishi gave two paragraphs to somebody and asked for the middle: the first paragraph and the last, a hundred and fifty-two words and a hundred and forty-one, and nothing else, with the instruction to write the plan of the middle, one line a paragraph, as many lines as she thought there were, and she wrote eleven lines in about nine minutes and recovered eight of the twelve jobs. Ms Huda asked whether eight of twelve is a pass, and Mrs Khoury said it is the figure to aim at and the four she misses are the interesting half, because a pair of paragraphs from which a reader can reconstruct every paragraph has a middle that is doing nothing, and a pair from which they can reconstruct four is a pair that has not been written. Mr Shihabi asked which four she missed, and three, six, ten and eleven, of which three are the right answer: six and ten are the load-bearing paragraphs, which are invisible by design, and eleven is the paragraph cut in 23.5. Mrs Oueidat asked what paragraph 3 is, and that it establishes that the method predates the problem it is now used for, which is the second-largest claim in the chapter, and nothing in the first paragraph signals that the chapter has a historical half at all, so her supervisor found it and thought it was a digression. Dr Qabbani asked about the repair, and eleven words in the first paragraph, and the same reader a week later produced paragraph 3 as her second line. Mr Tayara asked what goes in the first paragraph, and Mrs Khoury said three things: what the piece is for, what a reader will be able to do at the end, and one thing it does not do, and the third is the one the room always leaves out. Mrs Rizk asked about the last, and what a reader may take, what they may not, and the one announced turn. Mrs Ajouz asked why the may not matters, and Mrs Khoury said because a reader with two minutes will use the piece for something and that sentence is the only control the writer has. Mr Attar asked how long the pair should be, and two hundred and ninety-three words for four thousand one hundred and sixty, which is about seven in a hundred. And Mrs Tarabishi said the thing she will take away is that the test is cheap and nobody does it: two paragraphs, one reader, nine minutes and eleven lines in somebody else\'s handwriting, and she has written four chapters, had every one read by three people, and never once asked anybody what they thought the middle of one was.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line of the report is the finding and it is about a whole profession.** **Two paragraphs, one reader, nine minutes, eleven lines in somebody else\'s handwriting** — **and she has written four chapters, had every one of them read by three people, and never once asked anybody what they thought the middle of one was.** **Ask the room who has ever run this test, and the answer in this class was nobody.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **A preamble as the first sentence** → **it could open any piece.**   **2** **No sentence saying what the piece does not do** → **the reader looks for what is not coming.**   **3** ⚠ **A last paragraph with no _may not_** → **the two-minute reader uses the piece for anything.**   **4** **The turning fact spent in the first paragraph** → **the middle has nothing left to turn on.**   **5** ⚠ **Telling the test reader how many paragraphs there are** → **the question is answered before it is asked.**   **6** **Every miss treated as a failure** → **two or three of them are the design.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **It says what the chapter asks; what a reader will be able to say at the end of it, which is on what the three studies can and cannot be compared; and one thing it does not do, which is propose a new measure** — **and it carries the eleven-word clause about the measure being older than the question, which is the repair the test produced.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **Paragraph 3, which establishes that the method predates the problem and is the second-largest claim in the chapter.** **The repair is eleven words in the first paragraph** — **and the same reader, a week later, produced paragraph 3 as her second line, so the recovery went from eight of twelve to nine of twelve, and the three she now misses are the correct three.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **At a hundred and sixty words the reader recovered four jobs.** **At four hundred she stopped reading the second paragraph properly, which showed because her last three lines were all about the recordings** — **and that is evidence of a reader\'s attention and not of their opinion, which is the only kind this test produces.**'));
