out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the eleventh mediation of Book 2 and the only one in which all three accounts are correct readings of three different amounts of text.** **The chairman read the first and last paragraphs and reported what they say.** **The supervisor read the chapter in twenty minutes and found the claim where the load-bearing paragraph had made it readable.** **The writer has a one-page document that would settle the whole thing in forty seconds and has never sent it, which is 20.7 in a different profession.** ⚠ **Three things follow and the room should get them in this order.** **First: the plan is the repair for the twenty-minute reader and no use at all to the other, so the repair for him is 23.6 and that is why 23.6 comes before this cycle.** **Second: the plan goes clipped to the front of the piece and addressed to nobody, because a plan sent to one reader is a submission in an argument.** **Third, and the hardest: a load-bearing paragraph moves the centre of gravity of a piece one paragraph to the right, which is a cost of 23.4 that nobody mentioned when it was taught** — **and the repair is a clause in the earlier paragraph and not the removal of the later one.** **And then Pair B, where everything was sent and read and the piece is four thousand words for a reader who asked for fifteen hundred: the architecture is faultless and the brief was ignored, which is 23.5 with somebody else\'s number.**'));
